      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 130,
      "outputTokens": 885,
      "costUsd": 0.00803,
      "pronunciationText": "So, each month, a little more of the repayment goes towards the debt. The repayment stays the same, but the interest is charged on a smaller balance each time. Less interest means more of the six hundred dollars is left to reduce the balance, and that makes the next balance smaller again. This works because six hundred dollars is more than the interest. If it weren't, the balance wouldn't fall. Now run all five years. By the end, the interest is only ay few dollars a month. The sixtieth repayment is just three hundred and seventy-four dollars eighty-one, and the car is paid off."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-06",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-f09/scene-06.wav",
